  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nNo significant weather impacts are expected over the next week\nplus. The pattern will continue to be mainly dry with high \npressure dominating for the bulk of the forecast period. Only \nexceptions will be this afternoon and evening and again on \nThursday as dry cold fronts push through the region. These \nfronts will bring an increase in wind gusts, however they will \nalso bring an increase in relative humidity, which alleviates \nsome of our fire weather concerns. Wind gusts of 15 to 20 mph \nwill be possible starting this afternoon as the pressure\ngradient over the region tightens some. Minimum RH will range \nin the upper 40s to low 50s percent in the mid afternoon, before\nincreasing in the late afternoon and evening as the stronger \nwind gusts arrive. Due to the increase in RH, we are not \nexpecting to issue any fire related special weather statements, \nbut will continue to monitor fire weather conditions closely.\n\nSome of the guidance does bring a few showers or sprinkles\nacross the area with the cold frontal passage later today and\nlater Thursday, but the rainfall potential is very low and\nspatial coverage will be limited.",
